1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,033
But what do we know
about human presence
inside the Grotta del
Bue Marino? The cave

2
00:00:06,033 --> 00:00:11,121
is not only a place
of extraordinary
natural beauty: it
is also a site

3
00:00:11,121 --> 00:00:15,845
of great
archaeological
interest. The evidence
gathered indicates

4
00:00:15,845 --> 00:00:21,805
that the cave was
frequented as far back
as prehistoric times.
Looking at the wall

5
00:00:21,805 --> 00:00:26,820
in front of you, you
can see a panel
of particularly
significant rock

6
00:00:26,820 --> 00:00:31,326
carvings:
approximately twenty
anthropomorphic
figures incised

7
00:00:31,326 --> 00:00:37,068
by hammering, a cupule
— a small circular
hollow carved into the
rock — and two

8
00:00:37,068 --> 00:00:42,737
concentric circles
with a further cupule
at the centre.
According to scholars,

9
00:00:42,737 --> 00:00:47,970
these carvings may
date back to the
Ozieri Culture
(4000–3500 BC) or the

10
00:00:47,970 --> 00:00:53,421
Filigosa Culture
(3000–2500 BC),
certainly at a time
when the sea level was

11
00:00:53,421 --> 00:00:59,381
several metres lower
than today and access
to the cave most
likely took place from

12
00:00:59,381 --> 00:01:05,268
the land. If you look
towards the lower
right, you will notice
an isolated figure

13
00:01:05,268 --> 00:01:10,501
with a distinctive
"candelabra"
structure, equipped
with multiple limbs.

14
00:01:10,501 --> 00:01:15,952
Just above it, two
figures can be made
out with oblique
lateral appendages.

15
00:01:15,952 --> 00:01:21,912
Higher still, there
is a figure with arms
and legs bent in the
same direction. All

16
00:01:21,912 --> 00:01:27,654
the figures display
elongated, curved, and
inclined limbs,
arranged irregularly

17
00:01:27,654 --> 00:01:32,669
around the concentric
motifs. The most
widely accepted
interpretation

18
00:01:32,669 --> 00:01:38,047
suggests that the
scene depicts a group
of praying figures
arranged around

19
00:01:38,047 --> 00:01:43,280
a circular symbol,
perhaps connected
to religious rituals
or to symbolic

20
00:01:43,280 --> 00:01:47,714
practices related
to nature. Similar
stylised
anthropomorphic

21
00:01:47,714 --> 00:01:53,311
representations can
also be found at other
Italian sites, such
as the Naquane

22
00:01:53,311 --> 00:01:58,908
National Rock Carving
Park, the Sas Concas
site, and the Grotta
del Papa. The

23
00:01:58,908 --> 00:02:04,214
similarities between
places that are
geographically very
distant from one

24
00:02:04,214 --> 00:02:09,811
another suggest the
existence, in ancient
times, of a shared
cultural network

25
00:02:09,811 --> 00:02:15,771
in which symbols and
ritual practices
spread among different
communities. What you

26
00:02:15,771 --> 00:02:21,368
are looking at is not
merely carvings
in rock: you are
looking at a testimony

27
00:02:21,368 --> 00:02:26,674
to the prehistoric
spirituality
of Sardinia. At this
precise spot, nature

28
00:02:26,674 --> 00:02:32,560
and human presence
meet, leaving a mark
that spans thousands
of years of history.
